<Layout title="Methodology" active="Method"><div class="frame"><div class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow"><i></i> RESEARCH NOTES <b>/</b> THIS EDITION</div><h1>Methodology</h1><p>How to read an institutional classification, check a number, and understand the limits of a comparison.</p></div><div class="subbody prose"><section id="personal-trace"><h2>Tracing your own work</h2><p>Enter a workplace and a title to find a profession. Common job titles and aliases lead to a profession card. Where several fields fit, you choose one. The initial trace runs in your browser tab. If you choose Look up sources, Door Notes receives the employer and job title for that request. It sends the employer name to participating public indexes and uses the role to check relevance. The search service does not store the submitted fields. You can clear your entries. Shared links and images contain only the public profession record.</p><p>The trace follows the institutions that shaped access for Black professionals. Employer-specific links appear only when a cited source documents a connection for that workplace and profession. A training partnership describes an institutional relationship. Your personal hiring and training history would need its own evidence.</p></section><section id="suggestions"><h2>Choosing a workplace and title</h2><p>The suggestion fields search directories downloaded to your browser. The first line names the actual workplace. When a source identifies a school district, university system or other affiliation, it appears underneath. Choose the specific site you work at or keep the text you entered. A listing identifies an organization; workplace history and awards require their own evidence.</p><p>Job-title suggestions include terms from the O*NET® 31.0 Database, U.S. Department of Labor, Employment and Training Administration. The vocabulary is adapted under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. See <a href="https://www.onetcenter.org/database.html">the O*NET database</a>, <a href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-open/blob/main/data/JOB-TITLES.md">title sources and attribution</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-open/blob/main/data/WORKPLACES.md">workplace-directory sources</a>. Your typed words remain usable when a listing is missing. The optional federal-recipient search sends the workplace text to USAspending only after you click its button.</p></section><section id="timeline-method"><h2>How the timeline connects the evidence</h2><p>Your preview shows the earliest sourced opening in the profession record, the strongest available evidence for your workplace, and the connections still missing. Open the full timeline to see the other milestones and their sources.</p><p>Field history names a specific institution, the change it made and the date its source supports. Workplace steps identify a published relationship or award for the employer and role. A date range stays a range. We label missing event dates and distinguish publication or index dates from event dates.</p><p>Chronology shows order. It does not establish a chain of cause. A named institutional connection has its own citation. A gap marks where a link to your employer or your individual hiring route is unknown. Your current job is attributed to your entry. The profession-wide description of entry today is labeled editorial.</p><p>The first timeline can use dated, locally indexed awards without sending your entries to a publisher. Choosing Look up sources checks for more evidence. Documented results update the timeline; research leads remain below for review.</p></section><section id="source-discovery"><h2>Live employer research</h2><p>You can research an employer outside the reviewed directory and a job title outside the profession index. Each result links to the publisher or its source record. A documented award identifies the recipient and connects funding to institutional access or training in the field. Funding establishes what was awarded. Hiring or training outcomes require their own evidence. A research lead needs closer reading before drawing that connection. Live results are separate from reviewed editorial entries and national measurements.</p><p>The service searches public education and research records, plus recent news coverage when available. Public indexes have uneven coverage. Each live result links back to its source. Where an earlier dated index supplies a result, the page labels it separately. An empty result means this search found no matching record. An unavailable index is labeled separately. A wider web search is optional and sends the displayed employer and role terms to the search engine.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-open">Open-source code, data and attribution ↗</a></p></section><section><h2>Origin and gate</h2><p>Origin identifies institutions that helped build access to a profession for Black Americans. Gate identifies institutions proposed as influencing entry today. The seed classifications come from the project brief. Each rationale explains the decision, supporting evidence and its limits. A proposed label is awaiting editorial review.</p><p>A profession can list several institutions. The role lookup uses the first-listed origin and gate. Institutional influence can coexist with a change in representation. These observations do not establish causation.</p></section>
<section id="job-titles"><h2>How job titles become paths</h2>
<p>Door Notes matches your wording against published professions and the occupations listed for each title in O*NET. When a title has several meanings, you can choose the duties closest to your work. For example, a systems administrator may manage networks or databases.</p>
<p>A related field can offer historical context. Each historical event and each connection to a workplace needs its own source. Charts follow the occupation defined by their publisher. Job matching runs in your browser.</p>
<a href="https://www.onetcenter.org/dictionary/31.0/json/job_titles.html">O*NET job title definitions ↗</a></section>
<section id="exploring"><h2>How career suggestions work</h2>
<p>The explorer uses the activities you choose and the tasks in five published occupation records. It shows where a job uses those activities and links you to the original task descriptions. You can skip either question, compare different roles, or change your answers.</p>
<p>The Career map brings together occupation duties, Black field history and possible next steps. The detailed guides cite the Bureau of Labor Statistics for advancement. An occupation directory entry can identify duties even where we have not reviewed its history or a transition. Its responsibility examples describe the kind of work someone does. Local grades and promotion decisions require evidence from the employer. Each Door Note identifies what its historical source establishes and what is still open.</p>
<p><a href="/explore/">Try the activity picker →</a> · <a href="/career-map/">Compare IT moves →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Who is counted</h2><p>Each series follows its publisher’s definition of race, sex, occupation, geography and denominator. The default comparison uses the source’s Black male and Black female categories. Birthplace is unrestricted unless the definition explicitly says otherwise.</p><p>Workforce share uses everyone employed in the matched occupation as its denominator. A training-entry percentage uses the corresponding student cohort. The historical civilian labor force may also include experienced unemployed workers. Choose the relevant measure on each card.</p><p>Percentages use one decimal for display. Charts use straight segments between consecutive comparable observations. The 2003 marker flags a federal race-reporting change. Each source’s actual boundaries apply, and a change of definition or a missing year breaks a line. A single point is one observation.</p><p>Counts come from published tables or weighted survey estimates. ACS estimates use person weights. Where available, the source data include survey sampling uncertainty. The chart does not extrapolate back to 1990.</p></section>
<section><h2>Institutions in the taxonomy</h2><dl>{codes.map(c=><><dt>{labels[c]}</dt><dd>{descriptions[c]}</dd></>)}</dl></section><section id="role-matrix"><h2>The role matrix</h2><p>The brief supplies five specific sentences. Other pairs show the generic match or difference sentence. Open any origin to see every gate. These sentences describe the editorial classification.</p>{codes.map(a=><details class="reading"><summary>{labels[a]}</summary><dl>{codes.map(b=><><dt>{labels[b]}</dt><dd>{roles[a+':'+b]||(a===b?'Origin and gate match':'Origin and gate differ')}</dd></>)}</dl></details>)}</section>
<section id="corrections"><h2>Report an error</h2><p>Use the correction form on GitHub. Identify the profession, source table or editorial classification, describe the issue and link the supporting evidence. The tracker is public.</p><a class="secondary" href="https://github.com/Fuzzyslippers412/pipeline-map-corrections/issues/new?template=correction.yml">Open the correction form ↗</a></section>
<section><h2>Edition notes</h2><p>Research preview · November 2026. Initial institutional assignments, verified source snapshots and a source register. Independent editorial reviews and editor sign-off are pending.</p><p>Door Notes is editorial analysis of public data and is independent of the publishers cited. This edition collects no visitor analytics.</p></section></div></div></Layout>
